#### 32bit references

For example, to specify a non-solid ref number of 32 bit hex:

```
 // [begin ref #50000000000000000000000000000000]
 {...}                in useRefToElectron(def);
 delRefFromElectron(beginIntralayer(50000000000000, def) end)
 [end reference #5000000000000000000000000000000]
 // [begin ref #]   [{name} \newline]
 {...}                {put or skip around}
 delRefFromElectron(endIntralayer(#, def)       \newline)
 [end reference #...   [{name} ends)]
 // [[/begin ref #...]]
 `
```

Note that the rest of the instructions and {..} tags should be omitted

#### 64bit references

For example, to specify that a reference contains 64 bit hex:

```
 // #500000000000000000000000000000000
 // [{name}                                 ]
 {tabType8}\\vertex\\angle=[a]            \\newline
 ...                      
 [\\newline]
 \newline
 (Here is what is being said after 500000000000000000000000000000000)\\newline
 the subscript \newline
 [\\newline]
 Mm9n and Gd are for the element Mm9n-Gd-11262
 {tabType8}addIdent eq Show the id of the solid (name) using the solid handler}  

```

Examples:

```
 // #5000000000000000000000000000000000
 // [{name}
 {"#" quickFit"SolidName"} produces read back of solid ref "quickFit"},
 // causes reference: #0000\0000000000000 quicklyFit
 {"#" quickFit"MoleculeWeight11262"} produces read back of molecular weight (get) "11262.000000"
 //}}
```

This allows one to read back properties of the given molecule in the calculations by usage of {solid name} above.

#### 	READING

The {read} tag is used for accessing a given atom, or a molecule,
from the POINT which this {read2} command is defined in.

```
 // [# ] is the reference  #500000000000000000000000000000000
 // {read}
 put x{ digits } into y{ digits }
 { \dots -> \csname one_or_zero_digit(y{ digits })# \endcsname puts x{ digits } \}
 \end{CSNT}
```

Where the POINT is #50000000000000000000000000000000.

#### CONVERTING

{convert} is used to unit convert an ENCODED floating number into an IMPLICIT symbolic value to be later placed on the plot with addCls. For example, one may want to use the value of the readback from the POINT defined above using {element}, or {subscript}. 

