In `> 1e6` and \begin{code}
< 1e-6
\end{code}, it's going to print out a lot of big numbers. Now `vrl` has some kind of into that you can't print, so it gets truncated.

There are also some increased risk to printing too big numbers. For example, in

\begin{code}
pr = clampf(0.000000,1.000000,vrl);
mkl = maxl(pr,k1);
\end{code}

you are multiplying with `maxl`. Since the result of that multiplication is an exponent, `clampf` is going to wrap around to 0 or 1 in these cases. This means now, offering `mkl` may be invalid. Since e.g.

\begin{code}
mkl = [[5 - 0]
      [5 1.241e-8]
      [5 0.000000]
      [5 3.800e-7]
      [5 0.000000]];
\end{code}

`[[5 - 0]]` evaluates to 0.000001799999999999999, which is unsigned, but `[[5 0.000000]]` is an int, even though it's less than `0.000000000000001`. So `mkl` is 0 now, but when this first instruction gets evaluated, `k1` will be 0, so that's undefined behavior.

\begin{code}
k1 = clampf(0.000000,1.000000,vrl);
\end{code}

Since `/0` is also undefined, that function call in `mkl = maxl(pr,k1);` is really `zero` in mathematical lingo, so you get an undefined result. So if you can avoid printing `vrl` in that way, you will be much more probable to get a proper result.

Besides the question about the rounding errors, I saw another one in

\begin{code}
h6p3 = 1./1.12803e+18/0.000179999999999999999;
k1 = abs(pr-k2);

tfr = [[
  4  0.3333333333333333   0.6666666666666666   0.0000000000000000
  4  0.5  0.1666666666666667   0.7500000000000000
  4  1.0  1.333333333333333   2.6666666666666668
  4  0.3333333333333333   0.6666666666666666   0.0000000000000000
  4    0  0.9666666666666667   0.2333333333333333]
\end{code}

As you can see, it's printing a fractional number (so coverted incorrectly) of 1/0.00000000000000002, which will give an invalid output when you divide by it. Despite this fact, you are doing `1/1.12803e+18/0.0001799999999999999999`. Go figure out for yourself why this happens. But it seems likely that 15 digits is not enough for truly exact printing.

Live programming without full live debugging is nevertheless possible, but bigger scripts (more than 15 lines) should be checked before use.

