Answer: You didn't specify if \strong{n extends to double} so I can give a general answer:

According to the [awk manual](https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/html_node/Integer-Numbers.html#Integer-Numbers) you have a subtle issue to deal with regardg decimal number parts.

You have these \strong{$NF} ("number of fields") values:
\begin{itemize}
\item $4: integer
\item $7: integer
\item $10: integer
\end{itemize}

It will match only if $4+1+10 or $7+1+10 or $10+1+10.

It also doesn't match `0000` (for instance) nor `000` (either two exactly two repeating digits or `00000X` or `00222Y`).

I recommend something like this three below:

\begin{code}
awk 'BEGIN { OFS="\t" }
print $0, (n ? " n" : ""), $NF, !( ERRCOUNT == 5 ) { ERRCOUNT++ }'
\end{code}

where `n` is used to indicate a decimal number. The `= 5` part makes sure that, if it reaches a "rightmost" solution, it will print "AND HAS IT!" since you have a problem only if this is the case based on an exact match description. Obtain two additional fields containing the number of decimal places (related to `n`):

\begin{code}
.*<</SIO/nSM><</SIOC>([<</SIO/nSM>>]|[<</SIOC>>])
\end{code}

Then, if the number of decimal places is not zero, a small modification:

\begin{code}
print $0 " Decimal number " $IFS, (n ? " " : "") i, (n && i) { (n+1)!i }
\end{code}

The last variable `i` is used to print the last insignificant digit.

\begin{code}
1.8234 Fe6 B1 Pb138 Br
 Decimal number   2    i   1
 Decimal number   2    i   1
 Decimal number   1    i   99
 Decimal number   1    i   99
 [/nSM>]<[/SIO]
 Decimal number   2    i   99
 Decimal number   2    i   99
\end{code}

