Answer: Assuming that you have no linebreaks in your input, then it is very easy, just convert the characters you want to convert and replace.

\begin{code}
sed -e 's/\/\([ ^]*\){2}[^^]*\//\1\1/g'
\end{code}

Or using `tr`

\begin{code}
sed 's/\/\([ ^]*\){2}[^^]*\//\1\1/g' | tr -d '/>'
\end{code}

This directly changes `\v` to `/`, followed by `\h` to the left, `\w` to a single character and then again `\h` to the right. `\{1,2}` This bracket means one or two occurs once matching the input character. `\\` preserves those `\` characters.

Case sensitive by default, so ...

\begin{code}
sed -i 's/\/\([ ^]*\){1,2}[^^]*\//\1\1/g'
\end{code}

Change `[^` to `[^`

A `sed` pure regex replace, using multipattern expressions to match wide whitespace, but you'll get better pure regex performance with `sed 2` IMO:

\begin{code}
sed -i 's/\/\(?[^ ]\){(?:\#\{4})[^^]*\//[/ | ]\1\1/g'
\end{code}

Those `[^]` and `[^ ]` are not same. `[^]` means any char \em{except} that listed, `^` means exact char.

Also, note that the default for the delimiter only has `+` (or match all) characters, so you'll need to use part of input to specify `+` to use a `\r` to replace the trailing space issue (because `a\b` matches a inside `ab`).

Same code with that fixed:

\begin{code}
sed -i 's/\/\(?[^ ]\){(?:\#\{4})[^^]*\//[/ | ]\1\1/g'
\end{code}

I couldn't test the performance yet, like always.

