Answer: According to your code, you first execute:

\begin{code}
Plan pl = new Main.Plan();
...
\end{code}

But you don't invoke the `Main` class itself. So that cannot work. While the below line can:

\begin{code}
Instance inst = pl.getRandomInstance();
\end{code}

`new Random()` generates a random object of the type `java.util.Random` (this follows the \em{usual suspects} rule, similar to when enums in java are instantiated). But in your case it would not make sense to do so to generate an `Array` of e.g. `node.class` with size 64.

Another thing could be missing - the constructor of `Plan` has the argument `Fact` - something you might need/use to properly create the `BrainTree`. EMPIES reports the seed as not valid:
`  java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Can't create Instance of Fact from null Seed. seed == null`

\begin{code}
    System.out.println(//declare it to a instance of class BrainTree);
    while(!(node.getRank() % 2 == 0)) {
        System.out.print("||");
        node = node.getLeft();
    }
    System.out.println();

    if (seed != null) {
        //chain the seed here
        seed = Plan.getInstance(node, null, (Fact)null);
    }
    else {
        seed = Plan.getInstance(node, al, active);
    }

    inst.setInstance(seed);
\end{code}

`(Fact) null` is not a valid and correct usage of null here. You can put the provided object in as an argument. When using `null` the getter method of the `BrainTree`; is called which will work until it throws an exception. Then an error is thrown by the `Instances.getInstance()` method. You would need to check if this throws an exception prior to calling `Instances.getInstance()`.

All in one might look something like:

\begin{code}
    Fact f = new Fact(seed);
    seed = Plan.getInstance(node, al, f);
    inst.setInstance(seed);
\end{code}

