How did Stackoverflow publish this suggestion?

Answer: Toolmakertoys discovered that Ghost, the open source blogging platform Stackoverflow uses to parse and render suggested responses, will pretty print a hash key, which is a string:

\begin{code}
{
  "type": "inst",
  "priority": 2,
  "desc": "<<SYS>>",
  "input": "2",
  "title": "Ac Ir Pd"
}
\end{code}

Substituting that generated hash key into the [instructions on Ghost's site](https://ghost.org/access/data/) produced the suggested method (The tool is the `etherpad` block editor).

On January 21, 2017, StackOverflow published the following articles in the answers to these questions: ["Why is the answer to this question being displayed in a <ghost> tag? (+ near identical ask.csv ama)](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/45569344/why-is-the-answer-to-this-question-being-displayed-in-a-ghost-tag-aka-near-identic/45569992#45569992) and ["How to hack the AMA widget – next to a ghost tag (-p admin)](http://stackoverflow.blog/2017/01/21/hack-the-ama-widget-next-to-a-ghost-tag-p-admin/):

\begin{blockquote}

The team at Ghost recently launched an access configurator allowing developers to secure their Ghost instance if they don’t have access to the server level: [Configure Security for Ghost in 5 Minutes](https://ghost.org/access/)..

Some of the security control instructions pertain to AMA widgets, so we leverage this suggestion system. The ghost tag removes the output from Ghost that is generated by the `suggestions` API hook, allowing you to see what we are seeing and adjust the output as needed. This line of output would block and/or truncate content, but you can customize Ghost.com to your liking!

  _{Source: https://stackoverflow.blog/2017/01/21/hack-the-ama-widget-next-to-a-ghost-tag-p-admin/#comment444940_45569992}

Result:

  <a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/9xkkq.jpg" rel="noreferrer"><IMAGE></a>
\end{blockquote}

Why does this ghost tag exists? Ghost thinks it's a secure suggestion system for Stack Overflow.

Answer: It seems it has to do with the syntax of Javascript, since Square Bracket Literals:

\begin{code}
\u{E000}: [ expected identifier, number, or string (at character number 66)
     \u{E001}: [ expected expression, pragmas (at character number 116)
     \u{E200}: [' or " expected (at character number 243)
\end{code}

Are all clearly shown in the output, as:

\begin{code}
 \u{E200}: [' or " expected (at character number 243)
\end{code}

